import{_ as a,o as t,c as o,ao as n}from"./chunks/framework.B31wclXz.js";const f=JSON.parse('{"title":"FFTW and FFTL","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"explanation/fftw.md","filePath":"explanation/fftw.md","lastUpdated":null}'),r={name:"explanation/fftw.md"};function i(s,e,h,l,d,F){return t(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="FFTW-and-FFTL" tabindex="-1">FFTW and FFTL <a class="header-anchor" href="#FFTW-and-FFTL" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;FFTW and FFTL {#FFTW-and-FFTL}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>FFTW, the &quot;Fastest Fourier Transform in the West&quot; by Matteo Frigo and Steven G. Johnson, is one of the most important pieces of numerical software in existence. For more than a quarter of a century it has been the FFT that scientific computing relies on, and the reference every new FFT is measured against, including this one. FFTL&#39;s name is a nod to it. This page describes what FFTW achieved and what FFTL takes from it.</p><h2 id="A-short-history" tabindex="-1">A short history <a class="header-anchor" href="#A-short-history" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A short history {#A-short-history}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The fast Fourier transform entered computing with the paper of Cooley and Tukey in 1965; the idea goes back to Gauss, as Heideman, Johnson and Burrus showed in 1984. For three decades afterwards, a fast FFT on a new machine meant hand-written code for that machine.</p><p>FFTW changed this. Frigo and Johnson started it at MIT in the late 1990s with an idea that was new for numerical libraries: instead of one implementation tuned by hand, FFTW combines many small, automatically generated kernels and lets a planner choose, at run time and on the actual machine, the combination that is fastest there (&quot;FFTW: an adaptive software architecture for the FFT&quot;, ICASSP 1998). The kernels, the codelets, come from <code>genfft</code>, a special-purpose compiler written in OCaml that derives straight-line code for small DFTs symbolically, simplifies it and schedules it for the register file (&quot;A fast Fourier transform compiler&quot;, PLDI 1999). The PLDI paper received the conference&#39;s Most Influential Paper Award ten years later, and FFTW received the J. H. Wilkinson Prize for Numerical Software in 1999.</p><p>FFTW 3, described in &quot;The design and implementation of FFTW3&quot; (Proceedings of the IEEE, 2005), added SIMD codelets, the real-to-real transforms with their eleven kinds, multithreading and the guru interface for arbitrary strides and batches. Along the way, Johnson and Frigo found a split-radix variant with fewer arithmetic operations than any power-of-two algorithm known since 1968 (&quot;A modified split-radix FFT with fewer arithmetic operations&quot;, IEEE Transactions on Signal Processing, 2007).</p><p>FFTW&#39;s interface became a standard of its own. MATLAB&#39;s <code>fft</code> functions are based on FFTW, and vendor libraries offer FFTW-compatible interfaces so that existing programs can switch to them. Julia shipped FFTW in its standard library until version 0.7; since then FFTW.jl provides it, and the conventions of FFTW.jl are the conventions of the Julia ecosystem.</p><p>That a library designed in the late 1990s is still the benchmark for every new FFT on every new processor is the clearest measure of what its two authors accomplished. It is an exceptional achievement, sustained over decades.</p><h2 id="What-FFTL-takes-from-FFTW" tabindex="-1">What FFTL takes from FFTW <a class="header-anchor" href="#What-FFTL-takes-from-FFTW" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What FFTL takes from FFTW {#What-FFTL-takes-from-FFTW}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>FFTL is written from FFTW&#39;s papers and documentation; FFTW&#39;s source code was not used. From these, FFTL takes:</p><ul><li><p><strong>Generated codelets.</strong> FFTL&#39;s symbolic generator writes straight-line DFT code for small radices, the idea of <code>genfft</code>. FFTL lets Julia&#39;s compiler do this when a radix and element type are first used, instead of generating C code ahead of time (see <a href="/FFTL.jl/previews/PR9/explanation/algorithms#Algorithms">Algorithms</a>).</p></li><li><p><strong>The real-to-real transforms.</strong> The eleven kinds, their names (<code>REDFT10</code>, <code>RODFT00</code>, …), their unnormalised definitions and the halfcomplex order are FFTW&#39;s, so that code and formulas written for FFTW carry over unchanged.</p></li><li><p><strong>The interface.</strong> Signs, scalings and function names follow FFTW.jl. libfftl, the C library, implements FFTW3&#39;s C and Fortran interfaces from the FFTW manual; the interface is FFTW&#39;s design.</p></li></ul><h2 id="Where-FFTL-goes-another-way" tabindex="-1">Where FFTL goes another way <a class="header-anchor" href="#Where-FFTL-goes-another-way" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Where FFTL goes another way {#Where-FFTL-goes-another-way}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>FFTL has no run-time planner. The radix sequence of a length comes from a fixed rule found by offline searches on the development machines, and the sequence is part of the plan&#39;s type, so Julia compiles the whole transform for it. Plans are therefore reproducible and need no wisdom, at the price of compiling each new radix sequence once. Stockham chains take the place of the decompositions FFTW&#39;s planner chooses, multidimensional transforms run without transposition, and one code base serves CPUs, GPUs and the C library. On the measured machines FFTL is faster in many cases and FFTW in others (<a href="/FFTL.jl/previews/PR9/reference/benchmarks#Benchmarks">Benchmarks</a>); FFTW is always measured at its best setting (<code>MEASURE</code>, built with native CPU flags).</p><h2 id="References" tabindex="-1">References <a class="header-anchor" href="#References" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;References {#References}&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><p>J. W. Cooley and J. W. Tukey, &quot;An algorithm for the machine calculation of complex Fourier series&quot;, <em>Mathematics of Computation</em> 19 (1965), 297–301.</p></li><li><p>M. T. Heideman, D. H. Johnson and C. S. Burrus, &quot;Gauss and the history of the fast Fourier transform&quot;, <em>IEEE ASSP Magazine</em> 1(4) (1984), 14–21.</p></li><li><p>M. Frigo and S. G. Johnson, &quot;FFTW: an adaptive software architecture for the FFT&quot;, <em>Proc. ICASSP</em> 1998, vol. 3, 1381–1384.</p></li><li><p>M. Frigo, &quot;A fast Fourier transform compiler&quot;, <em>Proc. ACM SIGPLAN PLDI</em> 1999, 169–180.</p></li><li><p>M. Frigo and S. G. Johnson, &quot;The design and implementation of FFTW3&quot;, <em>Proceedings of the IEEE</em> 93(2) (2005), 216–231.</p></li><li><p>S. G. Johnson and M. Frigo, &quot;A modified split-radix FFT with fewer arithmetic operations&quot;, <em>IEEE Transactions on Signal Processing</em> 55(1) (2007), 111–119.</p></li><li><p>FFTW website and manual: <a href="https://www.fftw.org" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">fftw.org</a>.</p></li></ul>',15)])])}const m=a(r,[["render",i]]);export{f as __pageData,m as default};
